9
00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:30.599
What you will build: the matching engine of a B2B produce exchange.

10
00:00:30.879 --> 00:00:43.999
Wholesalers and kirana buyers post bids and farmers and aggregators post offers for standardised lots (onion grade A, 10-tonne lots), and the engine matches them by price and time priority.

11
00:00:44.279 --> 00:00:53.879
It is the last and most exotic case study, and it gathers the handbook's themes: data structures on the hot path;. a single-writer design for determinism;.

12
00:00:55.229 --> 00:01:05.405
event sourcing for recovery and replicas;. risk checks inside the critical path;. fan-out of a replicated state (market data) that must never be silently wrong.

13
00:01:05.685 --> 00:01:10.378
This is the design a stock exchange or order matching system interview question.

14
00:01:10.658 --> 00:01:11.725
The four labs:

15
00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.394
L1: order books (real, measured).

16
00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:25.426
All three implementations produced the identical trade stream (1,752,321 trades, same hash) from 3M events:

17
00:01:26.776 --> 00:01:33.261
A sorted list managed 14k events per second, with a 540 µs p99.

18
00:01:33.541 --> 00:01:37.531
A TreeMap of price levels managed 3.5M per second.

19
00:01:37.811 --> 00:01:44.680
A tick-ladder array managed 6.1M per second, with a 0.51 µs p99.

20
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.947
L2: determinism (real threads and journal).

21
00:01:48.227 --> 00:01:57.485
Threads matching under a lock produced 3 different trade streams from the same input in 3 runs, with nothing recorded that could explain or replay them.

22
00:01:57.765 --> 00:02:08.987
With a sequencer + journal + single matcher, replicas and crash recovery reproduced the live book exactly in 3 of 3 runs, at 4.0M orders per second.

23
00:02:10.337 --> 00:02:13.238
L3: pre-trade credit checks (simulation).

24
00:02:13.518 --> 00:02:21.454
Gateway-cached exposure let 392 accounts exceed their limits, by up to 1.96 lakh rupees.

25
00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:27.153
A central risk service was exact, but added 138 ms at p99.

26
00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:30.505
An in-path check was exact and cost microseconds.

27
00:02:30.785 --> 00:02:33.067
L4: market data (simulation).

28
00:02:33.347 --> 00:02:42.073
Unsequenced deltas left 18 of 200 subscribers with silently wrong books, and books were wrong 9.4% of the time.

29
00:02:43.423 --> 00:02:50.356
Sequenced deltas with retransmission gave 0 wrong books and 0.33% known-stale time.

30
00:02:50.636 --> 00:02:56.033
Snapshot-only recovery gave 0 wrong books, but 7.8% stale time.

31
00:02:56.313 --> 00:02:57.295
Reading time:

32
00:02:57.575 --> 00:02:59.367
110 min + code.

