34
00:03:01.743 --> 00:03:03.706
Regulatory scope (VERIFY).

35
00:03:03.986 --> 00:03:08.103
Exchanges for securities and commodity derivatives are regulated.

36
00:03:08.383 --> 00:03:17.258
In India, that means SEBI rules for stock and commodity exchanges; e-NAM and state APMC rules govern agricultural produce trading.

37
00:03:17.538 --> 00:03:26.413
This chapter designs a hypothetical B2B spot marketplace with exchange-style matching and makes no claim about the licensing it would need.

38
00:03:27.763 --> 00:03:28.616
Part 1.

39
00:03:28.896 --> 00:03:30.816
Introduction and Why It Matters.

40
00:03:32.066 --> 00:03:34.733
A story: the auction that couldn't explain itself.

41
00:03:35.013 --> 00:03:39.471
The grocery platform's sourcing team ran morning auctions for produce lots.

42
00:03:39.751 --> 00:03:43.677
The first version was a Spring service with a REST endpoint per order.

43
00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:50.485
Orders were matched in a @Transactional method that loaded resting orders from PostgreSQL with SELECT...

44
00:03:50.765 --> 00:03:54.413
FOR UPDATE ORDER BY price, createdat and wrote the trades back.

45
00:03:54.693 --> 00:03:59.962
At 7:00 am, when 800 buyers bid for the day's onion lots:

46
00:04:00.242 --> 00:04:01.223
It fell over.

47
00:04:01.503 --> 00:04:09.525
Row locks serialised everything. p99 went to seconds, and timeouts made clients retry, which added more orders.

48
00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:12.645
Nobody could explain an outcome.

49
00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:23.592
A buyer asked why their earlier bid at the same price lost to a later one. createdat came from four app servers with slightly different clocks, and the lock order differed from both.

50
00:04:23.872 --> 00:04:27.435
No record existed of the order the engine actually used.

51
00:04:27.715 --> 00:04:31.064
A buyer overspent their credit line by 4 lakh rupees.

52
00:04:31.344 --> 00:04:34.245
Each app server checked credit against a cached balance.

53
00:04:35.595 --> 00:04:36.918
The price screen lied.

54
00:04:37.198 --> 00:04:40.377
Dashboards built books from a WebSocket feed of changes.

55
00:04:40.657 --> 00:04:46.566
A dropped message left a phantom bid at 28 rupees or kg on some screens all morning.

56
00:04:46.846 --> 00:04:52.115
Sellers accepted lower prices because they believed a 28 rupeesbid existed.

57
00:04:52.395 --> 00:05:01.825
A matching engine is small (a few thousand lines), but it is where fairness, determinism, latency and correctness are all non-negotiable at once.

58
00:05:03.175 --> 00:05:03.857
Quote:

59
00:05:04.137 --> 00:05:05.801
The thesis of this chapter.

60
00:05:06.081 --> 00:05:08.257
The book is a data-structure problem.

61
00:05:08.537 --> 00:05:13.444
Price levels indexed by tick, and FIFO queues with O(1) cancel (L1).

62
00:05:13.724 --> 00:05:17.479
The engine is a single-writer state machine behind a sequencer.

63
00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:24.543
The sequenced journal is the truth, and determinism gives replicas, recovery and auditability for free (L2).

64
00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:28.215
Risk checks belong in the sequenced path, on in-memory state.

65
00:05:28.495 --> 00:05:31.908
Caches and remote calls make them wrong or slow (L3).

66
00:05:32.188 --> 00:05:34.385
Market data is replicated state.

67
00:05:34.665 --> 00:05:43.433
Sequence numbers, retransmission and snapshots make a subscriber's book either correct or known to be stale, never silently wrong (L4).

