113
00:07:24.647 --> 00:07:25.521
Part 2.

114
00:07:25.801 --> 00:07:27.679
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

115
00:07:28.929 --> 00:07:31.937
Intuition: an auctioneer with a numbered ticket roll.

116
00:07:32.217 --> 00:07:38.510
In a busy market hall, every bid is written on a ticket torn from a single numbered roll: that's the sequencer.

117
00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:50.523
One auctioneer processes the tickets strictly in number order, matching the best price first and the earliest ticket first at the same price: that's the single-threaded engine with price-time priority.

118
00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:54.302
A clerk copies every ticket into a ledger: that's the journal.

119
00:07:54.582 --> 00:08:00.150
If the auctioneer faints, a colleague replays the ledger and arrives at exactly the same state.

120
00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:06.787
Before accepting a bid, the auctioneer checks the buyer's credit on the board beside them, not a note from yesterday.

121
00:08:07.067 --> 00:08:14.278
The price board outside is updated line by line with numbered updates, so anyone who missed a number knows they're out of date.

122
00:08:15.628 --> 00:08:16.588
Requirements.

123
00:08:16.868 --> 00:08:17.721
Functional.

124
00:08:18.001 --> 00:08:26.300
Order types: limit, market, cancel, modify (cancel or replace); time-in-force (day, IOC, FOK); minimum lot sizes.

125
00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:32.340
Price-time priority matching per instrument (for example, ONION-A-NSK-10T-D1).

126
00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:39.511
Pre-trade risk: credit limits for buyers, inventory attestations for sellers, price bands (circuit breakers).

127
00:08:39.791 --> 00:08:43.247
Market data: top of book, full depth, trades, snapshots.

128
00:08:43.527 --> 00:08:47.729
Post-trade: trade confirmations, settlement instructions, invoices.

129
00:08:49.079 --> 00:08:50.189
Non-functional.

130
00:08:50.469 --> 00:08:55.845
Deterministic and auditable: every outcome explainable from a durable, ordered record.

131
00:08:56.125 --> 00:09:03.421
Latency: order-to-ack p99 in the low milliseconds end to end (the engine itself in microseconds).

132
00:09:03.701 --> 00:09:08.266
Throughput: bursts of tens of thousands of orders per second at auction open.

133
00:09:08.546 --> 00:09:14.455
Availability: failover within seconds, without losing or duplicating acknowledged orders.

