207
00:13:21.744 --> 00:13:23.429
The arrival race is real.

208
00:13:23.709 --> 00:13:28.317
Different runs interleave differently, and no design removes that at the gateways.

209
00:13:28.597 --> 00:13:34.485
What the sequencer changes is that the race's outcome becomes a fact: a numbered, durable journal.

210
00:13:34.765 --> 00:13:37.603
Everything after it is a pure function of the journal.

211
00:13:37.883 --> 00:13:50.704
Single-threaded matching per instrument group removes locks and nondeterminism (hash iteration order, time-dependent logic and thread scheduling are all banned from the matcher), and is fast enough (L1).

212
00:13:52.054 --> 00:13:56.918
Replicas consume the same journal and hold identical state (hot standby).

213
00:13:57.198 --> 00:13:59.374
Recovery is snapshot + replay.

214
00:13:59.654 --> 00:14:01.041
Audits replay any day.

215
00:14:01.321 --> 00:14:12.585
Journal durability and replication: the sequencer replicates each entry before acknowledging it (Raft or a primary-backup pair), with group commit (Ch 54 L2).

216
00:14:12.865 --> 00:14:18.347
The pattern is known as LMAX architecture or state-machine replication (Ch 12).

