506
00:35:49.725 --> 00:35:50.663
Part 6.

507
00:35:50.943 --> 00:35:52.223
Production Concerns.

508
00:35:53.473 --> 00:35:54.625
Failure modes.

509
00:35:54.905 --> 00:35:56.249
Here is a quick table:

510
00:35:56.529 --> 00:35:58.812
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

511
00:35:59.092 --> 00:36:00.457
Linear scans in the book.

512
00:36:00.737 --> 00:36:01.548
Symptom:

513
00:36:01.828 --> 00:36:03.492
Latency spikes (L1).

514
00:36:03.772 --> 00:36:04.775
Mitigation:

515
00:36:05.055 --> 00:36:07.039
Indexed levels, O(1) cancel.

516
00:36:07.319 --> 00:36:08.812
Multi-threaded matching.

517
00:36:09.092 --> 00:36:09.903
Symptom:

518
00:36:10.183 --> 00:36:13.233
Unexplainable, unreplayable outcomes (L2).

519
00:36:13.513 --> 00:36:14.516
Mitigation:

520
00:36:14.796 --> 00:36:16.524
Sequencer + single writer.

521
00:36:16.804 --> 00:36:18.660
Nondeterminism in the matcher.

522
00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:19.751
Symptom:

523
00:36:20.031 --> 00:36:21.439
Replica divergence.

524
00:36:21.719 --> 00:36:22.721
Mitigation:

525
00:36:23.001 --> 00:36:25.369
Ban clocks, randomness and hash order.

526
00:36:25.649 --> 00:36:27.484
Diff live versus replay daily.

527
00:36:27.764 --> 00:36:28.788
Cached risk.

528
00:36:29.068 --> 00:36:29.879
Symptom:

529
00:36:30.159 --> 00:36:31.844
Limit breaches (L3).

530
00:36:32.124 --> 00:36:33.127
Mitigation:

531
00:36:33.407 --> 00:36:34.452
In-path risk.

532
00:36:34.732 --> 00:36:36.033
Remote risk calls.

533
00:36:36.313 --> 00:36:37.124
Symptom:

534
00:36:37.404 --> 00:36:39.132
Tail latency (L3).

535
00:36:39.412 --> 00:36:40.415
Mitigation:

536
00:36:40.695 --> 00:36:41.740
In-path risk.

537
00:36:42.020 --> 00:36:43.641
Unsequenced market data.

538
00:36:43.921 --> 00:36:44.732
Symptom:

539
00:36:45.012 --> 00:36:47.145
Silently wrong screens (L4).

540
00:36:47.425 --> 00:36:48.428
Mitigation:

541
00:36:48.708 --> 00:36:51.353
Sequence numbers, retransmit, snapshots.

542
00:36:52.703 --> 00:36:53.962
Slow subscriber.

543
00:36:54.242 --> 00:36:55.053
Symptom:

544
00:36:55.333 --> 00:36:56.847
Backpressure into the engine.

545
00:36:57.127 --> 00:36:58.130
Mitigation:

546
00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:01.034
Decouple; conflate; drop and resnapshot.

547
00:37:01.314 --> 00:37:02.317
Engine crash.

548
00:37:02.597 --> 00:37:03.407
Symptom:

549
00:37:03.687 --> 00:37:05.309
Downtime, lost orders.

550
00:37:05.589 --> 00:37:06.591
Mitigation:

551
00:37:06.871 --> 00:37:09.666
Hot standby on the same journal, fenced promotion.

552
00:37:09.946 --> 00:37:11.055
Opening surge.

553
00:37:11.335 --> 00:37:12.146
Symptom:

554
00:37:12.426 --> 00:37:13.983
Chaos in the first seconds.

555
00:37:14.263 --> 00:37:15.266
Mitigation:

556
00:37:15.546 --> 00:37:17.594
Call auction, gateway rate limits.

557
00:37:18.944 --> 00:37:20.032
Observability.

558
00:37:20.312 --> 00:37:28.525
Engine: p50 or p99.9 or max per event type (HDR histograms), queue depths, GC pauses.

559
00:37:28.805 --> 00:37:32.219
Sequencer: journal replication lag, commit latency.

560
00:37:32.499 --> 00:37:36.701
Determinism: live versus replica state hashes every N events.

561
00:37:36.981 --> 00:37:41.568
Risk: rejections by reason, exposure utilisation, kill-switch events.

562
00:37:41.848 --> 00:37:47.459
Market data: gap rates per subscriber, retransmit requests, snapshot requests, lag.

563
00:37:48.809 --> 00:37:50.174
Security and fairness.

564
00:37:50.454 --> 00:37:55.894
Authenticated sessions, per-participant order-rate limits, and cancel-on-disconnect options.

565
00:37:56.174 --> 00:37:59.630
Equal treatment: no participant bypasses the sequencer.

566
00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:04.454
Gateway placement and latency are made uniform where fairness requires it.

567
00:38:04.734 --> 00:38:09.555
Audit trail: the journal plus output events, retained per regulation (VERIFY).

568
00:38:10.905 --> 00:38:11.801
Performance.

569
00:38:12.081 --> 00:38:15.132
Keep the engine single-threaded and allocation-free.

570
00:38:15.412 --> 00:38:18.249
Scale by partitioning instruments across engines.

571
00:38:18.529 --> 00:38:23.841
Separate stages (gateway, sequencer, engine, publisher) with SPSC ring buffers.

572
00:38:24.121 --> 00:38:29.561
Most latency is outside the engine: network, serialisation, journal replication.

573
00:38:30.911 --> 00:38:31.637
Cost.

574
00:38:31.917 --> 00:38:33.986
Modest hardware for the engine itself.

575
00:38:34.266 --> 00:38:38.426
The market-data fan-out and the journal replication are the scaling costs.

576
00:38:38.706 --> 00:38:42.055
Standby engines double compute, and are cheap insurance.

577
00:38:43.405 --> 00:38:44.344
Part 7.

578
00:38:44.624 --> 00:38:46.331
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

579
00:38:47.581 --> 00:38:48.498
Watch out:

580
00:38:48.778 --> 00:38:49.418
1.

581
00:38:49.698 --> 00:38:51.874
Matching in the database with row locks.

582
00:38:52.154 --> 00:38:53.647
It collapses at the open.

583
00:38:54.997 --> 00:38:55.915
Watch out:

584
00:38:56.195 --> 00:38:56.899
2.

585
00:38:57.179 --> 00:38:59.419
Multi-threaded matching on a shared book.

586
00:38:59.699 --> 00:39:02.301
Nondeterministic and unauditable (L2).

587
00:39:03.651 --> 00:39:04.569
Watch out:

588
00:39:04.849 --> 00:39:05.595
3.

589
00:39:05.875 --> 00:39:08.009
Wall-clock time inside the matcher.

590
00:39:08.289 --> 00:39:09.611
Replicas diverge.

591
00:39:10.961 --> 00:39:11.879
Watch out:

592
00:39:12.159 --> 00:39:12.863
4.

593
00:39:13.143 --> 00:39:14.636
Linear scans for cancel.

594
00:39:14.916 --> 00:39:16.601
Tail latency (L1).

595
00:39:17.951 --> 00:39:18.869
Watch out:

596
00:39:19.149 --> 00:39:19.917
5.

597
00:39:20.197 --> 00:39:21.882
Allocation-heavy hot paths.

598
00:39:22.162 --> 00:39:24.551
GC pauses become latency spikes.

599
00:39:25.901 --> 00:39:26.819
Watch out:

600
00:39:27.099 --> 00:39:27.845
6.

601
00:39:28.125 --> 00:39:29.725
Risk checks against caches.

602
00:39:30.005 --> 00:39:31.691
Limit breaches (L3).

603
00:39:33.041 --> 00:39:33.958
Watch out:

604
00:39:34.238 --> 00:39:34.985
7.

605
00:39:35.265 --> 00:39:37.526
Synchronous remote risk calls per order.

606
00:39:37.806 --> 00:39:39.534
Tail latency (L3).

607
00:39:40.884 --> 00:39:41.801
Watch out:

608
00:39:42.081 --> 00:39:42.764
8.

609
00:39:43.044 --> 00:39:45.647
Market-data deltas without sequence numbers.

610
00:39:45.927 --> 00:39:47.783
Silent corruption (L4).

611
00:39:49.133 --> 00:39:50.050
Watch out:

612
00:39:50.330 --> 00:39:51.077
9.

613
00:39:51.357 --> 00:39:53.703
Letting slow subscribers slow the engine.

614
00:39:53.983 --> 00:39:56.181
Couples everyone to the slowest client.

615
00:39:57.531 --> 00:39:58.448
Watch out:

616
00:39:58.728 --> 00:39:59.432
10.

617
00:39:59.712 --> 00:40:01.419
No reference implementation.

618
00:40:01.699 --> 00:40:04.259
Optimised books without a correctness oracle.

619
00:40:05.609 --> 00:40:06.526
Watch out:

620
00:40:06.806 --> 00:40:07.638
11.

621
00:40:07.918 --> 00:40:11.417
Continuous matching from the first second of a volatile open.

622
00:40:11.697 --> 00:40:13.147
Use an opening auction.

623
00:40:14.497 --> 00:40:15.415
Watch out:

624
00:40:15.695 --> 00:40:16.484
12.

625
00:40:16.764 --> 00:40:18.343
Failover without fencing.

626
00:40:18.623 --> 00:40:20.287
Two engines both publishing.

627
00:40:21.637 --> 00:40:22.511
Part 8.

628
00:40:22.791 --> 00:40:24.135
Interview Q and A.

629
00:40:25.385 --> 00:40:26.217
Beginner.

630
00:40:26.497 --> 00:40:27.521
Question 1.

631
00:40:27.801 --> 00:40:30.724
Design an order matching engine, main components?

632
00:40:31.004 --> 00:40:32.369
Thirty-second answer:

633
00:40:32.649 --> 00:40:48.841
Gateways (auth, validation, rate limits), then a sequencer that assigns a global order and writes a replicated journal, then a single-threaded matching engine per instrument group with in-memory books and risk, then output events for acks, trades and market data,

634
00:40:49.121 --> 00:40:57.377
then sequenced market-data feed with retransmit and snapshots, then post-trade settlement; a hot standby replays the same journal.

635
00:40:58.727 --> 00:40:59.730
Question 2.

636
00:41:00.010 --> 00:41:02.741
What data structure do you use for the order book?

637
00:41:03.021 --> 00:41:04.386
Thirty-second answer:

638
00:41:04.666 --> 00:41:18.490
Per side, price levels indexed by tick (array within a price band, tree fallback), each level a FIFO of orders with intrusive doubly-linked lists; an id, then order map for O(1) cancel; best-price pointers.

639
00:41:18.770 --> 00:41:23.229
Insert O(1)/O(log n), cancel O(1), match O(1) per fill.

640
00:41:24.579 --> 00:41:25.624
Question 3.

641
00:41:25.904 --> 00:41:27.611
What is price-time priority?

642
00:41:27.891 --> 00:41:29.256
Thirty-second answer:

643
00:41:29.536 --> 00:41:38.475
Orders match at the best price first; among orders at the same price, the one that arrived first (by sequence number, not wall clock) trades first.

644
00:41:38.755 --> 00:41:45.560
Modifications that worsen the order's position (larger size, new price) lose time priority by rule.

645
00:41:46.910 --> 00:41:47.977
Question 4.

646
00:41:48.257 --> 00:41:49.899
Why a sequencer and journal?

647
00:41:50.179 --> 00:41:51.545
Thirty-second answer:

648
00:41:51.825 --> 00:42:07.185
To turn the unavoidable race between concurrent submissions into a single durable order, so the engine's output is a deterministic function of it, enabling hot replicas, crash recovery by replay, audits and explanations of every trade.

649
00:42:08.535 --> 00:42:09.537
Intermediate.

650
00:42:09.817 --> 00:42:10.927
Question 5.

651
00:42:11.207 --> 00:42:12.721
Where should credit checks happen?

652
00:42:13.001 --> 00:42:14.367
Thirty-second answer:

653
00:42:14.647 --> 00:42:30.924
In the sequenced engine path on in-memory exposure updated by deterministic events (orders, fills, cancels, settlements, limit changes), exact and microseconds; gateway caches let accounts overspend, remote calls add queueing latency.

654
00:42:32.274 --> 00:42:33.383
Question 6.

655
00:42:33.663 --> 00:42:35.903
How do you make the engine deterministic?

656
00:42:36.183 --> 00:42:37.549
Thirty-second answer:

657
00:42:37.829 --> 00:42:56.495
Process only sequenced inputs in order on one thread; use the input's sequencer timestamp as time; no randomness, no wall clock, no iteration over unordered collections, no external calls; pure state machine; verify by replaying the journal and comparing state hashes with live.

658
00:42:57.845 --> 00:42:58.933
Question 7.

659
00:42:59.213 --> 00:43:02.051
How does market data stay correct for subscribers?

660
00:43:02.331 --> 00:43:03.696
Thirty-second answer:

661
00:43:03.976 --> 00:43:19.165
Sequence every message; subscribers detect gaps and mark books stale; a retransmit service fills small gaps and snapshots handle large gaps and late joiners; conflation for slow consumers; never let consumers slow the engine.

662
00:43:20.515 --> 00:43:21.539
Question 8.

663
00:43:21.819 --> 00:43:23.462
How do you fail over the engine?

664
00:43:23.742 --> 00:43:25.107
Thirty-second answer:

665
00:43:25.387 --> 00:43:42.155
A hot standby consumes the same journal and holds identical state; on primary failure, promote the standby with an epoch or fencing token so the old primary can't publish; clients reconnect; nothing acknowledged is lost because acks follow journal durability.

666
00:43:43.505 --> 00:43:44.572
Question 9.

667
00:43:44.852 --> 00:43:46.644
How do you test a matching engine?

668
00:43:46.924 --> 00:43:48.289
Thirty-second answer:

669
00:43:48.569 --> 00:44:09.476
A simple reference implementation as an oracle; replay recorded journals and diff trade streams and final books; property tests (no crossed book after matching, quantity conservation, priority invariants); fuzzing with random order sequences; performance tests reporting p99.9 with coordinated-omission-safe load.

670
00:44:10.826 --> 00:44:11.594
Senior.

671
00:44:11.874 --> 00:44:12.919
Question 10.

672
00:44:13.199 --> 00:44:15.311
Design a stock exchange end to end.

673
00:44:15.591 --> 00:44:16.957
Thirty-second answer:

674
00:44:17.237 --> 00:44:38.037
Gateways, sequencer with replicated journal, deterministic single-threaded engines partitioned by instrument, in-path risk, market data with sequenced deltas or retransmit or snapshots, hot standbys with fenced failover, opening or closing auctions, post-trade clearing and settlement, audit trail and surveillance, latency engineering.

675
00:44:39.387 --> 00:44:40.517
Question 11.

676
00:44:40.797 --> 00:44:42.760
How would you scale beyond one engine?

677
00:44:43.040 --> 00:44:44.405
Thirty-second answer:

678
00:44:44.685 --> 00:45:01.112
Partition instruments across engines (each single-threaded); gateways route by instrument; per-engine journals; cross-instrument features (multi-leg orders, shared credit) need coordination, pre-allocated credit per engine or a sequenced cross-engine protocol.

679
00:45:02.462 --> 00:45:03.550
Question 12.

680
00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:06.923
What are the latency engineering priorities in Java?

681
00:45:07.203 --> 00:45:08.569
Thirty-second answer:

682
00:45:08.849 --> 00:45:29.691
Single-threaded pinned hot loop; zero allocation on the hot path (pools, primitive maps, off-heap); SPSC ring buffers between stages; low-pause GC; batching of journal writes; measure with HDR histograms and coordinated-omission-aware tools; optimise network and serialisation, where most latency lives.

683
00:45:31.041 --> 00:45:32.321
Question 13.

684
00:45:32.601 --> 00:45:34.201
Why run an opening auction?

685
00:45:34.481 --> 00:45:35.847
Thirty-second answer:

686
00:45:36.127 --> 00:45:44.511
At the open, many orders arrive at once; continuous matching makes the first trades depend on microsecond arrival luck and thin books.

687
00:45:44.791 --> 00:45:52.641
A call auction collects orders for a period and computes one clearing price maximising matched volume, fairer and more stable.

688
00:45:53.991 --> 00:45:55.186
Question 14.

689
00:45:55.466 --> 00:45:58.133
How does post-trade work for a produce exchange?

690
00:45:58.413 --> 00:45:59.778
Thirty-second answer:

691
00:46:00.058 --> 00:46:17.850
Trade events, then confirmations, then settlement instructions (buyer pays from credit line or escrow, seller delivers to a warehouse or receiving point), then ledger entries (Ch 45), then invoices (GST), then quality disputes and adjustments as new events.

692
00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:20.096
Architect.

693
00:46:20.376 --> 00:46:21.571
Question 15.

694
00:46:21.851 --> 00:46:24.475
What must never happen, and how do you guarantee it?

695
00:46:24.755 --> 00:46:26.120
Thirty-second answer:

696
00:46:26.400 --> 00:46:46.773
Lost or duplicated acknowledged orders (ack after journal durability; idempotent client order ids), crossed books after matching (invariant checks), limit breaches (in-path risk), silently wrong market data (sequencing), divergent replicas (determinism + hash checks), two active engines (fencing).

697
00:46:48.123 --> 00:46:49.318
Question 16.

698
00:46:49.598 --> 00:46:52.606
How do you handle cancel-on-disconnect and kill switches?

699
00:46:52.886 --> 00:46:54.251
Thirty-second answer:

700
00:46:54.531 --> 00:47:09.401
Gateways detect session loss and submit sequenced mass-cancel events for opted-in participants; risk and ops kill switches are also sequenced events that cancel resting orders and block new ones, deterministic and replayable.

701
00:47:10.751 --> 00:47:12.095
Question 17.

702
00:47:12.375 --> 00:47:14.721
How is fairness ensured among participants?

703
00:47:15.001 --> 00:47:16.367
Thirty-second answer:

704
00:47:16.647 --> 00:47:30.833
Single sequencing point, equal access paths (uniform gateway latency where it matters), published matching rules, no privileged bypasses, rate limits per participant, auctions at volatile moments, and audits via replay.

705
00:47:32.183 --> 00:47:33.357
Question 18.

706
00:47:33.637 --> 00:47:35.173
Could you build this on Kafka?

707
00:47:35.453 --> 00:47:36.818
Thirty-second answer:

708
00:47:37.098 --> 00:47:56.597
Kafka can serve as the sequenced, replicated journal for a partition (one partition per instrument group), with a single consumer as the engine and output topics for events, fine for milliseconds-latency marketplaces like this one; ultra-low-latency exchanges use specialised sequencers and replication.

709
00:47:57.947 --> 00:47:59.184
Question 19.

710
00:47:59.464 --> 00:48:01.875
What does market surveillance need from the system?

711
00:48:02.155 --> 00:48:03.520
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:48:03.800 --> 00:48:18.712
The complete ordered input and output streams (orders, cancels, trades) with participant ids, to detect spoofing, wash trades and manipulation, best fed from the journal and output events into analytics with replay capability.

713
00:48:20.062 --> 00:48:21.171
Question 20.

714
00:48:21.451 --> 00:48:23.499
What makes a matching engine trustworthy?

715
00:48:23.779 --> 00:48:25.145
Thirty-second answer:

716
00:48:25.425 --> 00:48:41.745
Deterministic, replayable decisions from a single durable order; explainable priority; exact risk; correct-or-known-stale market data; fast enough with bounded tails; and continuous verification (replica hashes, invariants, reference diffs).

